1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:04,670
This is Jak Level Editor:
a level editor for Jak 3,

2
00:00:04,710 --> 00:00:07,560
built on the work of the OpenGOAL project.

3
00:00:08,158 --> 00:00:11,838
In the next minutes: what it is,
how it works, what you need,

4
00:00:11,878 --> 00:00:15,963
its main features, and two levels
made with it, played in the game.

5
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:21,125
First, a word about what this
project is, and what it is not.

6
00:00:21,735 --> 00:00:24,194
It is an alpha: a proof of concept.

7
00:00:24,234 --> 00:00:27,199
It is not meant to replace the
way levels are made today.

8
00:00:27,803 --> 00:00:32,495
OpenGOAL's modders build their levels in
Blender, and they do remarkable work.

9
00:00:33,103 --> 00:00:37,548
The idea here is different: to make
level editing accessible to everyone,

10
00:00:37,588 --> 00:00:41,888
without Blender, while showing the
level exactly as the game draws it.

11
00:00:43,583 --> 00:00:46,679
This project was built with
the help of an A I assistant.

12
00:00:46,719 --> 00:00:48,727
Many of its features and settings,

13
00:00:48,767 --> 00:00:52,442
in the editor and in the launcher,
were proposed by that A I.

14
00:00:53,059 --> 00:00:56,643
Some of them may well be too
complex, or not really usable,

15
00:00:56,683 --> 00:00:58,977
and removing them is being considered:

16
00:00:59,017 --> 00:01:02,482
for example, the adaptive
brightness of a placed element.

17
00:01:03,087 --> 00:01:06,247
So expect things to change,
and say what you find useful.

18
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:12,639
In short: this is a beta prototype,
version one point zero point one.

19
00:01:12,679 --> 00:01:15,583
It works, but it has rough edges and bugs.

20
00:01:16,178 --> 00:01:19,203
Keep copies of your work,
and report what goes wrong.

21
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:21,831
How does it work?

22
00:01:21,871 --> 00:01:23,939
Nothing of the game comes with the editor.

23
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:26,455
Everything comes from your
own copy of the game.

24
00:01:27,057 --> 00:01:30,230
The editor reads your Jak
3 disc with its extractor,

25
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:33,442
made from the data readers
of OpenGOAL's decompiler,

26
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:37,154
and writes every level once into
a library on your computer.

27
00:01:37,757 --> 00:01:40,214
From there come the
decor and its textures,

28
00:01:40,254 --> 00:01:42,916
the objects with their
positions and settings,

29
00:01:42,956 --> 00:01:45,305
and the light painted into every corner.

30
00:01:45,913 --> 00:01:48,339
What each object is, and what it can do,

31
00:01:48,379 --> 00:01:51,708
comes from the game's code,
as OpenGOAL decompiled it.

32
00:01:52,317 --> 00:01:56,984
And the prefabs: the editor finds whole
structures in your extracted levels,

33
00:01:57,024 --> 00:02:01,298
houses, towers, bridges, and makes
them the first time you show them.

34
00:02:03,017 --> 00:02:06,378
What you need: Windows 10 or 11, or Linux,

35
00:02:06,418 --> 00:02:10,022
in 64 bits, and a graphics
card with WebGL 2.

36
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:14,425
On Windows, WebView 2, which
Windows 11 already has.

37
00:02:15,013 --> 00:02:20,106
Your own copy of Jak 3 for the PlayStation
2, as a disc image or a folder.

38
00:02:20,146 --> 00:02:24,595
About 4 gigabytes of disk, and 5
of free memory while it extracts.

39
00:02:25,209 --> 00:02:30,318
Optionally, your copy of Jak two, for
its buildings: Haven City as it was.

40
00:02:30,917 --> 00:02:33,260
And to play what you build: jak project,

41
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:36,059
OpenGOAL's source code,
built on your computer.

42
00:02:36,099 --> 00:02:37,386
We will come back to it.

43
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:40,561
There are two ways to use it.

44
00:02:40,601 --> 00:02:43,487
On its own: download a
release from GitHub,

45
00:02:43,527 --> 00:02:47,133
an installer for Windows or
Linux, or a portable zip.

46
00:02:47,743 --> 00:02:50,963
The first time, it asks
where to keep your library,

47
00:02:51,003 --> 00:02:53,903
then for your game: File,
Extract game assets.

48
00:02:53,943 --> 00:02:58,820
A minute and a half later, the
272 levels of Jak 3 are ready.

49
00:03:00,535 --> 00:03:02,172
Or from the OpenGOAL Launcher.

50
00:03:02,212 --> 00:03:06,313
Its settings have a Level Editor page:
it installs a release of the editor,

51
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:08,340
and tells you when a new one is out.

52
00:03:11,997 --> 00:03:14,711
Its Level Editor button
then opens the editor,

53
00:03:14,751 --> 00:03:19,007
with a library of its own and the
discs the launcher already extracted:

54
00:03:19,047 --> 00:03:20,439
nothing more to choose.

55
00:03:22,354 --> 00:03:23,379
Here is the window.

56
00:03:23,419 --> 00:03:29,371
On the left, the panels: levels, building,
the catalog, prefabs and groups.

57
00:03:29,411 --> 00:03:31,197
On the right, the inspector.

58
00:03:31,237 --> 00:03:34,840
F1 opens the guide, with a
tutorial for every tool.

59
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:36,926
Let's open Spargus.

60
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:40,219
In the middle, the level is drawn
by the game's own renderer:

61
00:03:40,259 --> 00:03:42,419
what you see is what the game will show.

62
00:03:43,038 --> 00:03:47,555
Hold the right button
and fly with W, A, S, D.

63
00:03:47,595 --> 00:03:50,990
The wheel moves forward,
and F frames the selection.

64
00:03:52,935 --> 00:03:57,001
Point at an element: it is outlined,
and a label tells you what it is,

65
00:03:57,041 --> 00:03:59,036
and which structure it belongs to.

66
00:03:59,652 --> 00:04:02,723
Click to select it, then
drag its gizmo to move it.

67
00:04:02,763 --> 00:04:05,643
The editor shows where it
was, and how far it went.

68
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:08,515
A double click selects
the whole structure.

69
00:04:08,555 --> 00:04:13,114
Copy, paste, delete, group:
and everything can be undone.

70
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:18,552
The catalog holds what you can
place: the game's objects,

71
00:04:18,592 --> 00:04:23,793
interactive ones like crates, platforms and
doors, and its visual effects, by kind.

72
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:27,327
Pick one, click in the
level, and it lands there.

73
00:04:27,958 --> 00:04:31,042
The decor pieces of every
level, the game's models,

74
00:04:31,082 --> 00:04:33,226
and all its textures are there too.

75
00:04:35,157 --> 00:04:38,153
Prefabs are whole structures
taken from the game:

76
00:04:38,193 --> 00:04:40,631
houses of Spargus, towers of the desert,

77
00:04:40,671 --> 00:04:44,776
gates of the temple, and with Jak
two, the buildings of Haven City.

78
00:04:45,384 --> 00:04:47,722
The game's prefabs are only examples:

79
00:04:47,762 --> 00:04:50,848
a base to start from, made
from your own levels.

80
00:04:51,461 --> 00:04:52,535
You can make your own.

81
00:04:52,575 --> 00:04:57,608
Select what goes together, here a whole
house with Control G, then Create a prefab.

82
00:04:57,648 --> 00:05:00,798
It is saved in your
library, under My prefabs.

83
00:05:02,725 --> 00:05:06,168
The Build panel makes what the
game does not have: floors,

84
00:05:06,208 --> 00:05:10,652
walls with doors and windows, stairs
and roofs, in the style of any level.

85
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:14,140
The terrain brush paints
natural ground, hills,

86
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:18,006
valleys and cliffs, with the
textures of the level you choose.

87
00:05:19,932 --> 00:05:21,569
See your level as the game will.

88
00:05:21,609 --> 00:05:25,372
Change the hour, from dawn to
night, when the lamps light up...

89
00:05:25,991 --> 00:05:28,698
...and the weather, from
a clear sky to a storm.

90
00:05:30,635 --> 00:05:34,509
Levels can be opened side by side,
at their place in the world,

91
00:05:34,549 --> 00:05:36,724
and seen at any point of the story.

92
00:05:37,329 --> 00:05:41,681
Objects get behaviors: a door that
opens once a crate is broken,

93
00:05:41,721 --> 00:05:45,220
an elevator, or decor that
throws Jak into the air.

94
00:05:45,823 --> 00:05:49,976
And the hole finder looks for every gap
through which the player would see the void.

95
00:05:51,884 --> 00:05:53,509
Save your work as a project.

96
00:05:53,549 --> 00:05:57,896
Then export it for the game: the editor
writes a custom level for OpenGOAL,

97
00:05:57,936 --> 00:06:00,743
with a readme that gives
the exact lines to add.

98
00:06:01,347 --> 00:06:04,400
A level can also take the
place of one of the game's,

99
00:06:04,441 --> 00:06:07,534
with its music, its missions
and its continue points.

100
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:13,727
An A I assistant can build too,
through the editor's M C P server:

101
00:06:13,767 --> 00:06:18,346
you describe the place, it builds
it, checks for holes, and shows you.

102
00:06:20,050 --> 00:06:21,609
One important thing: for now,

103
00:06:21,649 --> 00:06:25,303
there is no automated way to test
your level right after making it.

104
00:06:25,343 --> 00:06:27,120
The editor does not run the game.

105
00:06:27,726 --> 00:06:30,924
To play it, my own fork of
jak project is recommended:

106
00:06:30,964 --> 00:06:33,962
its level builder has the
fixes these levels need.

107
00:06:34,562 --> 00:06:38,624
Copy the exported folders into it,
add the lines the readme gives,

108
00:06:38,664 --> 00:06:41,337
build the game, and
start it in your level.

109
00:06:43,050 --> 00:06:46,196
Now, let's put it into
practice, with two levels:

110
00:06:46,236 --> 00:06:49,813
one built from nothing, and
one of the game's, changed.

111
00:06:51,824 --> 00:06:53,890
First, a level built from nothing.

112
00:06:53,930 --> 00:06:58,616
A new level, and hills generated by the
terrain brush in the style of Spargus:

113
00:06:58,656 --> 00:07:02,595
the middle flattened into a valley,
the edges raised to close it.

114
00:07:03,203 --> 00:07:06,371
Then the game's prefabs,
each placed with a click:

115
00:07:06,411 --> 00:07:09,450
two shrines and the ruined
arches of the desert,

116
00:07:09,490 --> 00:07:14,697
a ruined tower of the arena, a tower of
Spargus, palms, and a Marauder watchtower.

117
00:07:15,684 --> 00:07:20,793
Crates, fires, and a stone disc: in
the inspector, it throws Jak up.

118
00:07:22,755 --> 00:07:25,679
The hole finder looks from
everywhere Jak could stand.

119
00:07:25,719 --> 00:07:29,812
What it finds here is up on the roofs and
the rocks, out of the player's reach.

120
00:07:30,412 --> 00:07:34,233
The level is saved as a project,
then exported for the game:

121
00:07:34,273 --> 00:07:37,040
its name, a nickname,
and where Jak starts.

122
00:07:39,950 --> 00:07:44,308
Into jak project now: my fork,
on a branch made for this demo.

123
00:07:44,348 --> 00:07:47,792
The export's two folders
are copied in, as they are.

124
00:07:48,394 --> 00:07:52,017
The readme gives the lines
to add: one in game dot G P,

125
00:07:52,057 --> 00:07:57,584
and the level's definition in level info
dot G C, with a number no other level has.

126
00:07:58,182 --> 00:08:01,710
Then goal C builds the game, and
the game starts in our level.

127
00:08:03,817 --> 00:08:05,454
And here it is, in the game.

128
00:08:05,494 --> 00:08:07,124
Jak arrives in the valley...

129
00:08:09,117 --> 00:08:13,356
...the shrines and the towers stand where
we put them, under the game's own sky...

130
00:08:16,617 --> 00:08:18,883
...and the stone disc
does what it promised.

131
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:24,746
Second, a level of the game, changed.

132
00:08:24,786 --> 00:08:27,347
We open Spargus, with its objects.

133
00:08:27,958 --> 00:08:31,865
On its square, a market of Jak
two's Haven City under its awnings,

134
00:08:31,905 --> 00:08:33,480
and a shrine of the temple.

135
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:36,045
The market's light is matched to Spargus.

136
00:08:36,651 --> 00:08:38,708
Crates, and another stone disc.

137
00:08:39,345 --> 00:08:43,646
Then, in the inspector, the level
becomes the replacement of Spargus:

138
00:08:43,686 --> 00:08:45,888
the game will load it in its place.

139
00:08:46,498 --> 00:08:50,234
Saved, and exported for the
game, under a name of its own.

140
00:08:55,917 --> 00:08:58,812
In jak project, the
folders are copied again,

141
00:08:58,852 --> 00:09:02,726
and one word changes: the nickname
of Spargus in level info.

142
00:09:02,766 --> 00:09:06,087
The game now loads our level
wherever it loaded Spargus.

143
00:09:08,083 --> 00:09:09,393
In the game, before...

144
00:09:11,383 --> 00:09:16,725
...and after: the market, the shrine,
the crates, on the square of Spargus.

145
00:09:17,783 --> 00:09:23,296
The creatures are left out, on purpose:
the editor builds places, not missions.

146
00:09:25,183 --> 00:09:28,686
And the market's stone disc
throws Jak above the rooftops.

147
00:09:30,783 --> 00:09:33,658
That was Jak Level
Editor, a beta prototype.

148
00:09:33,698 --> 00:09:37,436
Your own games, on your own machine:
that is what makes it work.

149
00:09:38,051 --> 00:09:40,068
Its code is open, on GitHub.

150
00:09:40,108 --> 00:09:43,050
Try it, and say what should
stay and what should go.

151
00:09:43,663 --> 00:09:48,573
Jak 3 belongs to Sony Interactive Entertainment,
and was developed by Naughty Dog.

152
00:09:48,613 --> 00:09:53,180
This editor is an independent project,
not affiliated with the OpenGOAL team.

153
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:54,299
Thanks for watching.
